Great project — and good instinct to keep this strictly to what your notes can support. A reviewer will respect that honesty.

I've grounded this plan only in your `SOR_scratch_notes.md` results, not the TODOs/groceries/seminar bits, and not in anything you didn't actually run.

```paper-plan
{
  "title": "Successive Over-Relaxation for the 2D Poisson Model Problem: Theory vs. Experiment",
  "style": "arxiv",
  "abstract_sketch": "We solve -laplacian(u)=1 on the unit square with Dirichlet 0 using the 5-point stencil (h=1/(n+1)) for n=16,32,64, comparing Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel (red-black), and SOR with optimal w*. We test the classical Young theory rho_J=cos(pi h), rho_GS=rho_J^2, w*=2/(1+sin(pi h)) against measured iterations to relative residual 1e-8, showing close agreement for Jacobi/GS and O(n^2) vs O(n) scaling.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "summary": "Motivate Poisson model problem and why classical iterations matter; state contribution: clean experimental check of Young theory at three grid sizes, no claim beyond n=64."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Model Problem and Discretization",
      "summary": "Define -laplace(u)=f on unit square, Dirichlet 0, f=1, 5-point stencil, h=1/(n+1), sizes 256/1024/4096 unknowns for n=16/32/64."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Iterative Methods and Convergence Theory",
      "summary": "Present Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel, SOR with red-black ordering (noting consistent ordering per Young); give formulas rho_J=cos(pi h), rho_GS=rho_J^2, w*=2/(1+sin(pi h)), rho_SOR=w*-1 and your w* values 1.6895, 1.8264, 1.9078; give predicted iters ln(1e-8)/ln(rho)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Experimental Setup",
      "summary": "u0=0, stop when ||r_k||_2/||r_0||_2 < 1e-8 in pure numpy; report exactly how red-black was vectorized; state n=128 was attempted but too slow and is excluded."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Results",
      "summary": "Table of measured iterations: n=16: 1064/543/65, n=32: 4020/2048/129, n=64: 15599/7948/261 for Jacobi/GS/SOR(w*); compare to predictions 1073/4059/15765, 537/2030/7883, 50/97/191; include log-log iterations vs n figure with expected slopes 2/2/1."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Discussion and Limitations",
      "summary": "Note Jacobi/GS match theory nicely while SOR takes ~30% more than asymptotic prediction — discuss asymptotic rate vs finite tolerance; explicitly state non-normal transient hump was NOT tested and will not be claimed."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Conclusion and Future Work",
      "summary": "Summarize O(n^2) vs O(n) win for optimal SOR; propose CG comparison and multigrid as future work only, plus overnight n=128 run."
    }
  ],
  "sources_needed": ["Young thesis 1950 + 1954 paper for optimal w* proof", "Varga Matrix Iterative Analysis for consistent ordering", "Golub & Van Loan Ch. 11 and LeVeque FD book for model problem background"]
}
```

This keeps you safe for review: no n=128 numbers, no transient claim, CG only as future work.

If this outline looks good, tell me "looks good, go ahead and write it" and I'll draft the full arXiv preprint from it. Want any changes — e.g. different emphasis on the SOR gap?

Perfect — keeping the SOR gap strictly open is the right call for a reviewer.

